; D3 as a MULTI-ARTIFACT MAP (issue #64 at the grain of issue #303).
;
; `examples/agreement-map/example.lara` is the same demonstration written as ONE
; file, and it stays in the tree byte-unchanged as the legacy oracle. Here the
; four papers are four separately authored, separately checkable artifacts —
; `paper-a/` .. `paper-d/`, each with its own declared `artifact` identity, its
; own co-located copy of this policy, and its own registered worked-example
; anchors — and this manifest is the only thing that composes them.
;
; Run it:
;
;     cabal run exe:lara -- check examples/agreement-map-multi/map.laramap
;     make map-check
;
; Each member alone accepts with its single claim `justified`. The composite is
; `contested, contested, justified, justified` — the same answer, on the same
; labels and edges, as the single-file oracle under the node correspondence
; pa=0, pb=1, pc=2, pd=3.
;
; The two edges the composite carries are the load-bearing part: NO member
; declares them, because no member can name another member's argument. They are
; generated by cross-member saturation from the declared contrary pair. And
; there is deliberately no `pc`/`pd` edge, because those two claims differ in
; the setting index.
;
; A map is a RECHECK, not a build: this file names where the members are and
; nothing about what they were. There is no checksum field and no lockfile, so
; editing a member changes the next verdict with no manifest update — see
; `docs/multi-artifact-composition-decision.md` D1. The `(artifact DIGEST)`
; each member declares is carried into the composite verdict as author-declared
; metadata, exactly as in a solo verdict; it is not verified against the bytes.
;
; The `alignments` below are the map-owned A3 rationale: each names BOTH sides,
; which is precisely what a member cannot do about a paper it does not contain.
; The checker verifies that each asserted `same`/`different` actually holds of
; the normalized propositions or of the selected argument coordinate; it does
; not verify the author's prose, and the `audit-status` is recorded rather than
; treated as evidence of review. Alignments neither create nor suppress an
; attack — they document and validate the atom choices that decide one.
;
; The paper contents and the declared identities are illustrative
; reconstructions, following the convention of every example in `examples/`.
;
; Write-up: docs/demos/d3-agreement-map.md.
(lara-map@1
  (policy agreement-v1 agreement-v1.policy.lara)
  (backends (backend nd 1))
  (members
    (member paper_a paper-a/example.lara)
    (member paper_b paper-b/example.lara)
    (member paper_c paper-c/example.lara)
    (member paper_d paper-d/example.lara))
  (alignments
    (alignment
      (ref paper_a c_pos whole)
      (ref paper_b c_neg whole)
      different
      (author alice)
      (audit-status reviewed)
      (rationale "P1 genuine disagreement: paper_a asserts better(...) and paper_b asserts not_better(...) over the same system, baseline, measurand and setting, so the two propositions are distinct and form a declared contrary instance."))
    (alignment
      (ref paper_a c_pos (arg 3))
      (ref paper_b c_neg (arg 3))
      same
      (author alice)
      (audit-status reviewed)
      (rationale "A3: the setting index D is identical (roberta_mnli_s60) on both sides. This is what makes the disagreement real rather than a comparison across different experiments."))
    (alignment
      (ref paper_c c_low (arg 3))
      (ref paper_d c_high (arg 3))
      different
      (author carol)
      (audit-status reviewed)
      (rationale "A3: the setting index D is DELIBERATELY distinct (bert_glue_s50 vs llama_openllm_s90). System, baseline and measurand agree; only the setting differs, so the prose disagreement is not an atom-level conflict and no attack is licensed."))
    (alignment
      (ref paper_c c_low (arg 0))
      (ref paper_d c_high (arg 0))
      same
      (author carol)
      (audit-status reviewed)
      (rationale "Both papers evaluate the same system, magnitude_pruning; the non-attack is due to the setting alone, not to a different subject.")))
  (questions
    (question q_comparability
      (refs (ref paper_c c_low whole) (ref paper_d c_high whole))
      (nl "What shared-setting bridging experiment would make paper_c and paper_d comparable? v0.1 has no cross-setting contrary, so the calculus refuses to manufacture the disagreement."))))

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; EXPECTED COMPOSITE VERDICT (human oracle; the machine one is map.verdict.sexp)
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;   (nodes  (node paper_a pa 0) (node paper_b pb 1)
;           (node paper_c pc 2) (node paper_d pd 3))
;   (labels (0 undec) (1 undec) (2 in) (3 in))
;   (edges  (0 1) (1 0))
;   (statuses paper_a c_pos contested,  paper_b c_neg contested,
;             paper_c c_low justified,  paper_d c_high justified)
;
; Identical labels, edges and statuses to `examples/agreement-map/expected.json`
; under pa=0, pb=1, pc=2, pd=3. The BYTES differ and are meant to: a composite
; verdict leads with `(scope map)` and reports each status under its
; `(member alias, claim name)` handle, so no consumer can mistake it for a solo
; `(verdict ...)`.
